  "question": {
    "text": "Chlorides of which metal are soluble in organic solvents:"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ca</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Mg</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Be</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>As per Fajan&#39;s rule, Be cations has a higher charge and smaller size, as a result, have a higher charge density and covalent nature.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ca</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Mg</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>&nbsp;are ionic compounds.</p><p>Hence, chlorides of Be will be more soluble in organic solvent.</p><p>&nbsp;</p>"
  }
